1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,643
Hi there! I'm Spud.

2
00:00:01,643 --> 00:00:05,279
We're Group 14, Team Sweet
Potatoes, and this is HearHere.

3
00:00:05,279 --> 00:00:08,585
Spud stands for Street Post
for Understanding Decisions.

4
00:00:08,585 --> 00:00:10,195
HearHere. Your street, your say.

5
00:00:11,195 --> 00:00:15,388
Melbourne's councils make decisions
about parks, housing and transport.

6
00:00:15,388 --> 00:00:19,644
Our fieldwork with eight international
residents found the barrier isn't a lack

7
00:00:19,644 --> 00:00:20,511
of interest.

8
00:00:20,511 --> 00:00:22,955
People can't find relevant information.

9
00:00:22,955 --> 00:00:26,535
Translated policies still use
terms they don't understand.

10
00:00:26,535 --> 00:00:28,614
And they don't know where
their feedback goes.

11
00:00:28,614 --> 00:00:31,821
Meanwhile, council news
arrives by mail and flyers,

12
00:00:31,821 --> 00:00:34,211
but newer residents live on Instagram.

13
00:00:34,911 --> 00:00:36,015
Meet Jason.

14
00:00:36,015 --> 00:00:38,910
He's 34, has lived in
Melbourne for eight years,

15
00:00:38,910 --> 00:00:41,361
and English isn't his first language.

16
00:00:41,361 --> 00:00:45,874
He's unhappy about a new park rule for
dogs, yet he doesn't know who to tell,

17
00:00:45,874 --> 00:00:47,164
or which words to use.

18
00:00:47,864 --> 00:00:52,622
Saturday, 8 a.m. Jason walks
his dog past a HearHere post.

19
00:00:52,622 --> 00:00:56,385
Its pillar shows verified
council and community notices.

20
00:00:56,385 --> 00:00:58,313
Hi! Got something to say? I'm all ears.

21
00:00:58,313 --> 00:01:01,605
The screen under Spud lists
nearby issues by distance,

22
00:01:01,605 --> 00:01:04,288
including a new rule
for the park next door.

23
00:01:04,988 --> 00:01:06,775
Jason taps the park issue.

24
00:01:06,775 --> 00:01:09,807
A plain-language summary
appears in his own language,

25
00:01:09,807 --> 00:01:11,800
with difficult terms explained.

26
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:14,856
Now he simply talks to
Spud, in his own language.

27
00:01:14,856 --> 00:01:18,552
The new park rule makes walking my dog
really inconvenient. I hope it can be

28
00:01:18,552 --> 00:01:19,404
reconsidered.

29
00:01:19,404 --> 00:01:24,105
Spud turns it into a clear English
comment, names the responsible department,

30
00:01:24,105 --> 00:01:26,462
and lets Jason edit before sending.

31
00:01:26,462 --> 00:01:27,798
No audio is stored.

32
00:01:28,698 --> 00:01:31,308
Jason taps his phone on the NFC pad.

33
00:01:31,308 --> 00:01:35,674
The issue and his comment move to the
HearHere app, and the post keeps nothing.

34
00:01:35,674 --> 00:01:38,447
Tracking begins only
because Jason chose it.

35
00:01:38,447 --> 00:01:41,190
The app shows each stage:
Received, In review,

36
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:44,928
then an outcome with a case
number, or reasons if declined.

37
00:01:44,928 --> 00:01:47,184
Notifications keep him updated.

38
00:01:47,184 --> 00:01:50,703
Each new HearHere post Jason
visits also earns a badge:

39
00:01:50,703 --> 00:01:52,687
a small reward for taking part.

40
00:01:53,587 --> 00:01:56,943
How does it all fit together?
Here's the post from every side.

41
00:01:56,943 --> 00:02:01,286
Spud's official social media account
shares only verified council posts,

42
00:02:01,286 --> 00:02:04,221
reaching residents where
they already scroll.

43
00:02:04,221 --> 00:02:08,534
An AI agent answers questions, and
the HearHere app tracks each issue.

44
00:02:09,334 --> 00:02:10,700
Not sure who to ask?

45
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:13,149
Spud's AI agent cites official sources,

46
00:02:13,149 --> 00:02:16,572
and hands over to a named
contact when it's unsure.

47
00:02:16,572 --> 00:02:20,862
And one tap shares a card on Instagram,
so Jason's neighbours can join in.

48
00:02:21,562 --> 00:02:25,820
For Jason, that means relevant issues
on his route, in his own language,

49
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:29,175
a clear department to hear
him, and visible progress.

50
00:02:29,175 --> 00:02:30,642
All without attending a meeting.

51
00:02:31,342 --> 00:02:32,745
HearHere has limits.

52
00:02:32,745 --> 00:02:36,460
Speaking in public may feel
exposing, AI can mistranslate,

53
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:39,690
and our fieldwork didn't
include council staff.

54
00:02:39,690 --> 00:02:44,342
Next, we'll test a low-fidelity prototype
with residents across languages.

55
00:02:45,042 --> 00:02:48,875
HearHere, with Spud: the Street
Post for Understanding Decisions.

56
00:02:48,875 --> 00:02:49,967
Speak here. Be heard!
